WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.540
Film Courage: When a filmmaker is making their
movie, what should they keep in mind while

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.860
making it and making sure they get a distribution
deal? Are there things that they should target

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:14.210
making the film, changing certain scenes that
they know distributors are going to want?

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:18.050
Let’s suppose they have their goldmine genre,
they realize what they’re making and who

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:22.289
that audience might be, but in terms of securing
a distribution deal, are there certain things

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:23.789
they should be aware of in making the film?

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:28.289
Scott Kirkpatrick, author: There are. A lot
of them aren’t the obvious things in the

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:35.750
sense of filmmakers for the most part tend
to focus on the movie, the movie, the movie,

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:40.380
and some of them (the more astute ones) will
definitely put some time into trying to find

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.970
a name talent to put into the film.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:48.900
But there’s a lot of paperwork and a lot
of other details that go into the delivery

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.920
process of getting a movie to a distributor
that become very important.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:01.780
Having all your agreements in place with your
talent, having all locations cleared, having

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:07.040
you know copyrights to everything but also
having things like music, cue sheets, transcripts,

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:13.250
there’s a lot of these fine details that
are in what are called material delivery lists

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.200
which are basically when you sign your movie
over all the stuff you have to give to the

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:26.200
distributors so they can extrapolate and make
any type of file spec any buyer might want,

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.870
you have to be able to give that to them so
they are not incurring any costs on the back

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.770
end. That’s really your job as the producer.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:38.460
The old vision is the old [Robert Rodriguez]
EL MARIACHI, you know I can make a movie for

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:44.320
$7,000-$8,000 and sell it and while that’s
true and that sort of happened, it was also

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:50.020
a long time ago and more importantly the company
that bought it had to invest a lot of money

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:55.060
in finishing it and completing it and getting
the audio fixed and getting the image fixed

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:58.740
and a lot of that stuff to make it market
ready.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.840
Companies aren’t ready to do that anymore
because the model of supply and demand. There’s

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:08.470
a ton of supply and there’s focused demand.
So maybe if you nail that demand, they’ll

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:13.510
help you out a bit but it’s not going to
be much and so you have to be aware of what’s

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:17.800
your responsibility to cover. And there’s
a lot of great resources on it if you just

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:18.940
Google around.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:23.560
Film Courage: Music cue sheets and transcription.
So with the transcription is someone just

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.480
transcribing it to English and then they’re
taking that file and then it can be easily

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.520
done into any other language?

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.900
Scott Kirkpatrick: Correct. Like in addition
to that, so it’s not the shooting script,

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:35.560
it’s actually the verbatim word-for-word,
what is said but more importantly there’s

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:42.440
timecode on the page so you have a ballpark
of what time in the movie it’s being said

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:49.470
and that’s for subtitling so that when they
translate all the subtitles (all those cues)

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:53.380
they know about where it’s supposed to hit
in the movie when they’re creating it.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.530
There’s also things like when you’re doing
the audio mix doing what they call M &amp; E tracks

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:01.920
where you separate out, you have your stereo
mixed tracks and then you have these other

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.130
two tracks that are all of the sounds and
music of the movie but if you’re watching

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:10.950
it and you just have those two audio tracks
playing, every single time somebody speaks

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:15.630
their mouth moves and no words come out that’s
because we’ve wiped out all of the audio

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:20.370
and the reason is so when you dub it in a
foreign language they only dub the audio,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:25.110
the verbal, they don’t have to dub the whole
movie and remix the whole movie they just

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:26.160
dump in the voice.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.690
Film Courage: Interesting.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:31.459
Scott Kirkpatrick: So you’ve got to make
it ready for that kind of stuff, that kind

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:33.270
of thinking because that’s the way distributors
think.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.370
Film Courage: And then the music cue sheet?
What would that be about?

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:40.400
Scott Kirkpatrick: The music cue sheet is
a lot of broadcasters internationally every

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:49.730
single time a music piece is used that is
affiliated with BMI, ASCAP, or one of the

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:57.510
unions (so to speak), the artist is given
their fair share of the royalty because their

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:00.790
music is being used to sell a product.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:06.099
So a lot of it is legal, a lot of it is documentation,
a lot of it is just stuff that you just kind

00:04:06.099 --> 00:04:11.500
of have to showcase…well you should have
it to protect you and your making the movie,

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:15.519
but also to protect the distributor so that
they know they can engage with your project

00:04:15.519 --> 00:04:18.639
in the marketplace and they’re not going
to have any issues later on.

